<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="grv-ch-questions">
  <title>Further problems</title>
  <introduction xml:id="grv-sec-questions-methods"><p>The zero-divisor constructions leave questions about other annihilators, the size of an <m>\LL</m>-equivalence class, and the behavior of generating series after changing coefficients. We collect these questions with the restrictions and examples established in the preceding chapters. The attributed conjectures retain the formulations in the cited versions; the questions about specified classes and families are directions for studying examples.</p></introduction>
  <section xml:id="grv-sec-status">
    <title>Regularity and additive structure</title>
    <p>Poonen's product relation and the affine-line annihilator give zero divisors in <m>\Rk</m>, and Ekedahl proves that zero divisors remain after localization; see <xref ref="grv-thm-poonen"/>, <xref ref="grv-thm-imou"/>, and <xref ref="grv-thm-ekedahl-localized"/>. Additive torsion and nilpotence ask for different equations.</p>
    <question xml:id="grv-q-torsion-nilpotence">
      <title>Additive torsion and nilpotence</title>
      <statement><p>For the usual ring <m>\Rk</m> over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field, consider the following questions.
        <ol>
          <li><p>Does <m>n\alpha=0</m>, with <m>n\ge1</m>, imply <m>\alpha=0</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Does <m>\alpha^N=0</m>, with <m>N\ge1</m>, imply <m>\alpha=0</m>?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p></statement>
    </question>
    <p xml:id="grv-anchor-dated-questions">The first is asked in <xref ref="grv-bib-LSZ" detail="Question 1.2(c)"/>; the second is formulated by Bot–Cangini–van Santen <xref ref="grv-bib-Bot"/>. Their positive-characteristic nilpotence construction concerns a Bittner presentation ring. Its relation to the ordinary scissors ring is the issue explained in <xref ref="grf-bittner-ring-warning" text="custom">the comparison with Bittner presentation rings</xref>, so that construction supplies no nilpotent in the ring considered here.</p>
    <p xml:id="margin-grv-dated-questions">For the first question, multiplication by the class of <m>n</m> points is regular exactly when there is no additive <m>n</m>-torsion. For the second, a nonzero nilpotent would have a nonzero power annihilating another power of itself. The product relations from the preceding chapters impose neither of these special forms on their two factors.</p>
    <p>For polynomials with constant coefficient <m>\pm1</m>, <xref ref="grv-prop-unit-constant"/> gives a more specific restriction: a nonzero annihilator lies in every <m>\LL^n\Rk</m> and cannot be <m>\LL</m>-power torsion.</p>
    <question xml:id="grv-q-unit-constant">
      <title>Regularity of polynomial classes</title>
      <statement><p>For a specified <m>f(t)\in\Z[t]</m> with <m>f(0)=\pm1</m>, is multiplication by <m>f(\LL)</m> injective on <m>\Rk</m>? In particular, are <m>[\PP^1]=1+\LL</m> and <m>[\mathbb G_m]=\LL-1</m> regular?</p></statement>
    </question>
    <p>The elementary reduction does not prove that <m>\bigcap_n\LL^n\Rk</m> vanishes. This intersection concerns divisibility in the original ring, rather than the dimension filtration of the localized ring. A nonzero element of this intersection annihilated by <m>f(\LL)</m> would still require its own construction.</p>
    <question xml:id="grv-q-family-regularity">
      <title>Regularity in a geometric family</title>
      <statement><p>Given a smooth projective family <m>\mathcal X\to S</m> over a finite-type complex scheme, describe the set of <m>s\in S(\C)</m> for which <m>[\mathcal X_s]</m> has a nonzero virtual annihilator. Which fibers have regular class?</p></statement>
    </question>
    <p>The elliptic construction in <xref ref="grv-ex-shioda-elliptic"/> provides a fiber with a nonzero annihilator. A statement about general or very general fibers would concern this set in a specified parameter space; it would be stronger than exhibiting one fiber, and would not follow from a cancellation theorem restricted to abelian varieties.</p>
  </section>
<section xml:id="grv-leq-problems">
    <title>Derived equivalence and annihilating exponents</title>
    <subsection xml:id="grv-q-derived-obstructions"><title>Derived equivalence and integral obstructions</title><p>The simply connected counterexamples in <xref ref="grv-leq-meinsma-counterexample"/> settle one direction of the comparison with derived categories. The opposite direction is the conjecture formulated in the February 2026 version of Meinsma’s paper <xref ref="grv-bib-Meinsma" detail="Conjecture 1.5"/>.</p><conjecture xml:id="grv-leq-hyperkahler-conjecture">
      <title>Meinsma</title>
      <statement><p>Let <m>X,Y</m> be complex projective hyperkähler manifolds. If <m>X\sim_{\LL}Y</m>, then <m>D^b(X)\simeq D^b(Y)</m>.</p></statement>
    </conjecture>
    <p>For K3 surfaces, the finiteness in <xref ref="grv-leq-efimov-finiteness"/> is complemented by <xref ref="grv-leq-meinsma-k3"/>: under its hypotheses, the members of the class are Fourier–Mukai partners. The conjecture asks for derived equivalence without those lattice restrictions and in higher dimensions.</p>
    <question xml:id="grv-leq-question-detection"><title>Integral obstructions and class relations</title><p>Given smooth projective complex varieties <m>X,Y</m> with matching Hodge–Deligne polynomials, which integral Hodge or Picard invariants distinguish their images in <m>\mathcal M_{\C}</m>? For a specified pair with isomorphic rational Chow motives, when can one construct a relation <m>\LL^N([X]-[Y])=0</m> geometrically?</p></question><p>Integral obstructions are supplied by <xref ref="grv-leq-efimov-rigidity"/> and <xref ref="grv-leq-meinsma-counterexample"/>. They distinguish pairs whose Hodge polynomials agree. In the other direction, a relation obtained from blowups or fibrations is an equality in the scissors ring and hence survives every realization.</p>
  <p>Equal rational Chow motives leave room for the first obstruction. For instance, nonisomorphic isogenous complex elliptic curves <m>E,E'</m> with <m>\End(E)=\Z</m> have isomorphic rational motives by <xref ref="grm-isogenous-elliptic"/>, but are not <m>\LL</m>-equivalent by Efimov's theorem. Thus an integral test can already separate localized classes within a family whose rational motives agree.</p></subsection><subsection xml:id="grv-q-uniform-exponents"><title>Bounds and uniform exponents</title><question xml:id="grv-leq-question-exponents">
      <title>Bounds on the exponent</title>
      <p>For a specified class <m>\mathcal C</m> of smooth projective varieties, which values of <m>\nu_{\LL}(X,Y)</m> occur for <m>\LL</m>-equivalent <m>X,Y\in\mathcal C</m>? Are these values bounded when the dimension is fixed? Does restricting the construction or deformation type give a bound?</p>
    </question><p>Minimal exponent one occurs for the K3 pairs in <xref ref="grv-leq-hassett-lai"/>. An equality with a larger exponent is not evidence that this exponent is minimal. There is a separate global ring question: does the chain
      <md number="no">0\subseteq\Ann(\LL)\subseteq\Ann(\LL^2)\subseteq\cdots</md>
      stabilize? It stabilizes exactly when one power of <m>\LL</m> annihilates all of <m>T_{\LL}(\Rk)</m>. Indeed, equality of two consecutive annihilators forces equality with every later one: if <m>\LL^{N+2}a=0</m>, first apply <m>\Ann(\LL^{N+1})=\Ann(\LL^N)</m> to <m>\LL a</m>, then to <m>a</m>. A bound for differences in one geometric class would be a weaker assertion. The nonzero first annihilator supplied by <xref ref="grv-thm-imou"/> gives no conclusion about strictness at later steps.</p>
    <question xml:id="grv-leq-question-families"><title>Least uniform exponent in a family</title><p>Let <m>\mathcal X,\mathcal Y\to S</m> be smooth projective complex families over a nonempty finite-type base, with <m>\LL</m>-equivalent fibers at every complex point. Determine the least integer <m>N\ge0</m> for which <m>\LL^N([\mathcal X_s]-[\mathcal Y_s])=0</m> for all <m>s\in S(\C)</m>. What bounds can be obtained from chosen relative polarizations or an explicit geometric construction?</p></question><p><xref ref="grf-uniform-family-exponent"/> proves that such an <m>N</m> exists, without giving a numerical bound or asserting constructibility of the minimal-exponent function. A relation valid over each member of a finite stratification gives an upper bound by taking the largest exponent. A theorem about a generic or very general member alone does not provide the hypothesis for all special fibers.</p><p xml:id="grv-zeta-organizing-problems">There is a related degree-dependent problem for a fixed <m>\LL</m>-equivalent pair <m>X,Y</m>: determine the minimal exponent for <m>\Sym^nX,\Sym^nY</m> as <m>n</m> grows. Put <m>r=\nu_{\LL}(X,Y)</m>. The bound <m>rn</m> in <xref ref="grv-sym-preserves-L-equivalence"/> gives coefficientwise equality after localization. A bound independent of <m>n</m> would give the stronger equality in <m>\Rk[[t]][\LL^{-1}]</m> described in <xref ref="grv-zeta-coefficientwise-localization" text="custom">the coefficientwise localization comparison</xref>.</p></subsection></section><section xml:id="grv-q-symmetric-powers"><title>Symmetric powers and rationality</title><p>Write <m>R=\Rk</m>, and use rationality at the origin as in <xref ref="grv-zeta-rational-definition"/>. The curve and surface results in <xref ref="grv-zeta-smooth-projective-curve"/> and <xref ref="grv-zeta-surface-criterion"/> concern the original ring. For a fixed variety outside the higher-dimensional obstruction in <xref ref="grv-zeta-shein-obstruction"/>, rationality in <m>R</m>, in <m>R/(\LL)</m>, and in <m>R[\LL^{-1}]</m> remain different tests. The example <xref ref="grv-zeta-obstruction-converse-fails"/> shows why the necessary geometric conditions alone do not settle the first test.</p><question xml:id="grv-zeta-fano-question">
      <title>Fano varieties modulo the affine line</title>
      <statement>
        <p>For a smooth projective Fano variety <m>X</m>, is the image of <m>\zeta_X(t)</m> in <m>(R/(\LL))[[t]]</m> rational? This is the algebraically closed characteristic-zero instance of the question posed in <xref ref="grv-bib-Shein25" detail="Question 4.2"/>. Its formulation uses the image of the series in the stable-birational quotient; it is weaker than rationality in <m>R[[t]]</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </question>

    <p>The question has elementary positive cases. A smooth projective rational variety is stably birational to a point, so <xref ref="grv-thm-larsen-lunts"/> identifies its class in <m>R/(\LL)</m> with <m>1</m>. By <xref ref="grv-sym-preserves-L-equivalence"/>, all its symmetric-power classes in this quotient are <m>1</m>, and its image series is <m>(1-t)^{-1}</m>. This verifies the case of projective spaces and, over <m>\C</m>, smooth del Pezzo surfaces. It does not give rationality of the original zeta function for an arbitrary rational variety in higher dimension: birational invariance of that assertion was proved here only for surfaces.</p>

    </section><section xml:id="grf-possible-comparisons"><title>Regularity and rings after base change</title><p>For an algebraically closed extension <m>K/k</m>, injectivity in <xref ref="grf-injectivity"/> reflects equalities between classes defined over <m>k</m>. A new annihilator over <m>K</m> may involve varieties with additional parameters. This gives a question about multiplication by one specified class.</p><question xml:id="grv-q-regular-basechange"><statement><p>If <m>\alpha\in R_k</m> is regular, is <m>b_{K/k}(\alpha)</m> regular in <m>R_K</m>? Equivalently, does <m>\operatorname{Ann}_{R_k}(\alpha)=0</m> imply <m>\operatorname{Ann}_{R_K}(b_{K/k}(\alpha))=0</m>?</p></statement></question><p>A finite certificate of <m>\alpha\beta=0</m> can be specialized, but the proof of injectivity does not ensure that a new witness <m>\beta</m> remains nonzero. Thus it does not settle this question. Units supply a positive case by <xref ref="grf-units-reflect" text="custom">the inverse calculation</xref>; a uniform theorem for other classes would require additional control of annihilators.</p><p>A separate comparison asks whether <m>R_k</m> and <m>R_K</m> are isomorphic as abstract rings, rather than whether base change is surjective or whether the rings share a few properties. <xref ref="grf-ring-cardinality"/> excludes an isomorphism for <m>\overline{\Q}</m> and <m>\C</m>. Comparing fields of equal cardinality lies beyond that argument.</p></section></chapter>
